2, 74&#x2013;98.e9. 10.1016/j.medj.2020.07.001</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.medj.2020.07.001</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7839961</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">33511375</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zheng L., Kelly C. J., Colgan S. P. (2015). Physiologic hypoxia and oxygen homeostasis in the healthy intestine. A review in the theme: Cellular responses to hypoxia. Am. J. Physiol. Cell Physiol. 309, C350&#x2013;360. 10.1152/ajpcell.00191.2015</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1152/ajpcell.00191.2015</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4572369</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26179603</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhou Q., Fu X., Dhital S., Zhai H., Huang Q., Zhang B. (2021). <i>In vitro</i> fecal fermentation outcomes of starch-lipid complexes depend on starch assembles more than lipid type. Food Hydrocolloids
120, 106941. 10.1016/j.foodhyd.2021.106941</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.foodhyd.2021.106941</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhu Y. Y., Thakur K., Feng J. Y., Zhang J. G., Hu F., Cespedes-Acu&#xf1;a C. L., et al. (2022). Riboflavin bioenriched soymilk alleviates oxidative stress-mediated liver injury, intestinal inflammation, and gut microbiota modification in b2 depletion-repletion mice. J. Agric. Food Chem. 70, 3818&#x2013;3831. 10.1021/acs.jafc.2c00117</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1021/acs.jafc.2c00117</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">35302755</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">36115405</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2022</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>23</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2022</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>23</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1879-1026</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>856</Volume><Issue>Pt 1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2023</Year><Month>Jan</Month><Day>15</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The Science of the total environment</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Sci Total Environ</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Coupling methanotrophic denitrification to anammox in a moving bed biofilm reactor for nitrogen removal under hypoxic conditions.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>158795</StartPage><MedlinePgn>158795</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2022.158795</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0048-9697(22)05894-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Simultaneous removal of ammonium and nitrate was achieved in a methane-fed moving bed biofilm reactor (MBBR). In the reactor, methanotrophic microorganisms oxidized methane under hypoxic conditions likely to methanol, hence providing an electron donor to denitrifiers to reduce nitrate to nitrite that then allowed anaerobic ammonium oxidizing bacteria (Anammox) to remove excess ammonium as N<sub>2</sub>. The ammonium and nitrate removal rates reached 72.09 &#xb1; 5.81 mgNH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>-N/L/d and 62.61 &#xb1; 4.17 mgNO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>-N/L/d when the MBBR was operated in continuous mode. Nitrate removal by the methane-fed mixed consortia was confirmed in a batch test revealing a CH<sub>4</sub>/NO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> molar removal ratio of 1.15. The functional populations were unveiled by FISH analysis and 16S rRNA gene sequencing, which showed that the biofilm was dominated by Anammox bacteria (Candidatus Kuenenia) and diverse taxa associated with the capacity for denitrification: aerobic methanotrophs (Methylobacter, Methylomonas, and unclassified Methylococcaceae), methylotrophic denitrifiers (Opitutaceae and Methylophilaceae), and other heterotrophic denitrifiers (Ignavibacteriaceae, Anaerolineaceae, Comamonadaceae, Rhodocyclaceae and Thauera). Neither DAMO archaea nor DAMO bacteria were found in the sequencing analysis, indicating that more unknown community members possess the metabolic capacity of methanotrophic denitrification.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2022 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xie</LastName><ForeName>Ting</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Materials and Environment, Guangxi Colleges and Universities Key Laboratory of Environmental-friendly Materials and New Technology for Carbon Neutralization, Guangxi Collaborative Innovation Center for Chemistry and Engineering of Forest Products, Guangxi Minzu University, Nanning 530006, China; Key Laboratory of Drinking Water Science and Technology, Research Center for Eco-environmental Sciences, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100085, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Xinyu</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Materials and Environment, Guangxi Colleges and Universities Key Laboratory of Environmental-friendly Materials and New Technology for Carbon Neutralization, Guangxi Collaborative Innovation Center for Chemistry and Engineering of Forest Products, Guangxi Minzu University, Nanning 530006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xu</LastName><ForeName>Yiming</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Materials and Environment, Guangxi Colleges and Universities Key Laboratory of Environmental-friendly Materials and New Technology for Carbon Neutralization, Guangxi Collaborative Innovation Center for Chemistry and Engineering of Forest Products, Guangxi Minzu University, Nanning 530006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bryson</LastName><ForeName>Samuel</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Washington, Seattle 98105, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Lu</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Washington, Seattle 98105, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Huang</LastName><ForeName>Kai</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Materials and Environment, Guangxi Colleges and Universities Key Laboratory of Environmental-friendly Materials and New Technology for Carbon Neutralization, Guangxi Collaborative Innovation Center for Chemistry and Engineering of Forest Products, Guangxi Minzu University, Nanning 530006, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><